CAT = "ccrnPsych"; SEG = "PSY"
Q = [
("Which tool screens a mechanically ventilated patient for delirium?",
 "CAM-ICU", ["Glasgow Coma Scale", "CPOT", "Braden scale"],
 "The Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU (CAM-ICU) and the ICDSC are validated delirium screens for patients who cannot speak. CPOT measures pain and the Braden scale measures pressure injury risk."),
("At which RASS score can the CAM-ICU not be completed?",
 "-4 or -5", ["0 (alert, calm)", "+1 or +2", "-1 or -2"],
 "The patient must respond to voice for the CAM-ICU to be done, so a RASS of -4 or -5 (responding only to touch, or not at all) is recorded as unable to assess, and arousal is rechecked later."),
("Which type of ICU delirium is most often missed?",
 "Hypoactive", ["Hyperactive", "Mixed with agitation", "Withdrawal-related"],
 "Quiet, withdrawn patients with hypoactive delirium are easy to mistake for calm or depressed, which is why routine screening with a validated tool is advised for every critically ill adult."),
("What does the 2018 PADIS guideline say about routine haloperidol to treat ICU delirium?",
 "It is not suggested", ["It is strongly recommended", "It is first-line for all", "It must be given with a statin"],
 "PADIS suggests against routine use of haloperidol, atypical antipsychotics or statins to treat delirium. Short-term use may still be considered for distress such as hallucinations or agitation that endangers the patient."),
("Agitation is keeping a ventilated patient from being weaned and extubated. Which drug does the PADIS guideline suggest?",
 "Dexmedetomidine", ["Lorazepam infusion", "Haloperidol", "Diphenhydramine"],
 "PADIS suggests dexmedetomidine for delirium with agitation that prevents weaning or extubation. Benzodiazepine infusions are linked to more delirium and longer ventilation."),
("After a patient's last drink, when do delirium tremens most often begin?",
 "48 to 96 hours", ["1 to 2 hours", "10 to 14 days", "4 to 6 hours"],
 "Early withdrawal signs such as tremor and anxiety start within about 6 to 24 hours, seizures peak around 24 to 48 hours and delirium tremens usually begin 48 to 96 hours after the last drink. A tool such as CIWA-Ar guides dosing."),
("A patient is shouting and threatening staff but has not touched anyone. What is the best first step?",
 "Speak calmly and keep space", ["Apply restraints right away", "Touch the arm to reassure", "Ask a series of questions"],
 "De-escalation uses a calm, low voice, short simple statements, respect for personal space and a clear exit for staff. Touch and a stream of questions can feel threatening; restraints are a last resort when there is imminent danger."),
("A patient recovering from an overdose seems hopeless. The nurse wonders about asking directly about suicide. What is true?",
 "Asking does not raise risk", ["Asking can plant the idea", "It is best left unasked", "Only psychiatry should ask"],
 "Asking directly and clearly about suicidal thoughts does not increase risk and is how risk is found. A positive answer leads to safety measures and a mental health evaluation."),
("Post-intensive care syndrome describes new or worse problems in which areas?",
 "Body, thinking and mental health", ["Muscle strength and nothing else", "Kidney and liver function", "Hearing and vision only"],
 "PICS covers weakness, memory and attention problems and anxiety, depression or PTSD after critical illness. Families can have their own version, PICS-F. Early mobility, sedation choices and family involvement help prevent it."),
("A patient on daily methadone for opioid use disorder is admitted after surgery. What is the usual plan?",
 "Continue the usual methadone", ["Stop it to prevent misuse", "Double it to cover the pain", "Replace it with naloxone now"],
 "The maintenance dose is confirmed with the treatment program and continued to prevent withdrawal, and acute pain is treated on top of it. Stopping it causes withdrawal; naloxone would reverse it."),
("A confused patient keeps reaching for an endotracheal tube. What should the nurse do before using restraints?",
 "Try less restrictive measures", ["Wait for the family to arrive", "Sedate the patient deeply first", "Restrain all four limbs"],
 "Restraints need an order, a documented reason and frequent reassessment, and less restrictive options come first: treating pain and delirium, covering lines, reorientation and close observation."),
("A patient with heart failure keeps refusing the fluid restriction. What should the nurse do first?",
 "Explore the reasons for it", ["Warn of the consequences", "Document the refusal only", "Ask security to step in"],
 "Nonadherence often has a reason, such as thirst, beliefs, cost or confusion about the plan. Finding it opens the way to solutions such as ice chips, mouth care or changes in the timing of fluids."),
]
